{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' conformance/factory/tickets/T-102.md && sed -n '1,280p' conformance/app/tests/health.test.js && sed -n '1,200p' conformance/app/package.json && git log --oneline --decorate -8 && git diff -- conformance/app/tests/health.test.js\"","aggregated_output":"# T-102 — /health reports approvals summary\n\nState: In progress\nBranch: ticket/T-102-health-approvals-summary\nRisk class: low (read-only endpoint, no external actions)\n\n## Description\n\nT-101 gave operators queue counts on `GET /health`. The other thing an operator checks at a glance is the approvals pipeline: how many proposed actions are waiting for a human, and how the closed ones ended. Extend `GET /health` to also report approvals counts by status.\n\nThe approval statuses in `conformance/app/server.js` are: `pending`, `sent`, `rejected`, `blocked_recipient`. The existing `ok` and `queue` fields must not change (T-101's tests stay green).\n\n## Acceptance criteria (planner freezes the exact contract)\n\n1. On a fresh server, `/health` reports zero approvals in every status. → satisfied when the response body matches Frozen contract §\"Fresh server\" exactly.\n2. After a job completes and proposes an action, `/health` shows one pending approval. → satisfied when the response body matches Frozen contract §\"One pending approval\" exactly.\n3. After that approval is approved, `/health` shows it under sent, and pending returns to zero. → satisfied when the response body matches Frozen contract §\"Approved\" exactly.\n\n## Frozen contract\n\n`GET /health` gains an `approvals` field alongside the existing `ok` and `queue` fields (unchanged, per T-101). Response is `200 OK`, `Content-Type: application/json`.\n\nShape:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"ok\": true,\n  \"queue\": { \"pending\": 0, \"done\": 0, \"dead\": 0 },\n  \"approvals\": { \"pending\": 0, \"sent\": 0, \"rejected\": 0, \"blocked_recipient\": 0 }\n}\n```\n\n- `approvals` has exactly these four keys, always present, in this order, values are non-negative integers counting `state.approvals` entries by `.status` (statuses defined in `conformance/app/server.js`: `pending`, `sent`, `rejected`, `blocked_recipient`).\n- No other keys are added to `approvals`. No existing key of the top-level response (`ok`, `queue`) changes shape or meaning.\n\nAcceptance criterion → exact expected body:\n\n1. **Fresh server** (AC1) — no events posted yet:\n   ```json\n   { \"ok\": true, \"queue\": { \"pending\": 0, \"done\": 0, \"dead\": 0 }, \"approvals\": { \"pending\": 0, \"sent\": 0, \"rejected\": 0, \"blocked_recipient\": 0 } }\n   ```\n\n2. **One pending approval** (AC2) — after posting an event and waiting for its job to reach `done` (which produces one approval in `pending`):\n   ```json\n   { \"ok\": true, \"queue\": { \"pending\": 0, \"done\": 1, \"dead\": 0 }, \"approvals\": { \"pending\": 1, \"sent\": 0, \"rejected\": 0, \"blocked_recipient\": 0 } }\n   ```\n\n3. **Approved** (AC3) — after `POST /api/approvals/:id/approve` on that approval (recipient on allowlist, so it succeeds):\n   ```json\n   { \"ok\": true, \"queue\": { \"pending\": 0, \"done\": 1, \"dead\": 0 }, \"approvals\": { \"pending\": 0, \"sent\": 1, \"rejected\": 0, \"blocked_recipient\": 0 } }\n   ```\n\nIf this contract proves wrong during build or test-authoring, the ticket goes back to Ready for re-planning as a new version — do not silently change the shape.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- App lives in `conformance/app/` (zero-dependency Node 18+; see `conformance/SPEC.md`).\n- Tests go in `conformance/app/tests/` — new file `tests/health-approvals.test.js`, same style as the existing suites (node:test, spawn the real server on its own port with a temp DATA_DIR).\n- Run tests from `conformance/app/` with `node --test tests/health-approvals.test.js`.\n\n## Log\n\n- 2026-07-12: ticket created by the operator; State → Ready. First ticket to be dispatched by Hermes (dispatcher trial).\n- 2026-07-12: planner froze `GET /health` `approvals` contract and mapped acceptance criteria to exact response bodies.\n- 2026-07-12 21:01:57: planner run completed through the wrapper; contract frozen in commit `8f0bc25`; State → In progress; cost $0.3306252.\n- 2026-07-12 21:06:49: test-author run completed through the wrapper; 3 contract tests committed in `18a7731`, all failing for the expected missing `approvals` field (0 passed, 3 failed); cost $0.2296.\n- 2026-07-12: builder implemented the frozen contract exactly (added `approvals` counts to the `/health` handler in `conformance/app/server.js`). `tests/health-approvals.test.js` (3/3) passes with exact `JSON.stringify` matches, confirming the implementation matches the frozen contract. **Blocked before commit:** this breaks all 4 pre-existing T-101 tests in `tests/health.test.js` (tests 1, 1b, 2, 3), which assert the `/health` body with `assert.deepStrictEqual(...)` against object literals that have no `approvals` key — `deepStrictEqual` rejects any extra key, so adding `approvals` anywhere in the response necessarily fails them. This is not an implementation bug: no server-side change can satisfy both \"exact frozen `approvals` shape per AC1–3\" and \"T-101's `health.test.js` stays green,\" because the latter was written before this contract existed and asserts an exact object with no extra keys. Per the builder's no-test-editing rule, I have not touched `tests/health.test.js` or any other test file, and have not committed the (correct, but suite-red) implementation change. Flagging for re-planning/reviewer adjudication: either (a) the contract stands and `health.test.js`'s 4 assertions need updating by the test-author/reviewer to tolerate/expect the new `approvals` key, or (b) the contract needs to change. Full suite as of this run: 11 passed, 4 failed (all 4 failures are the `health.test.js` ones described above; `health-approvals.test.js` and `conformance.test.js` are fully green).\n- 2026-07-12 21:10:01: builder run completed through the wrapper and committed implementation plus its ticket note in `5e201e0`; new contract tests pass 3/3 but the full suite is red (11 passed, 4 pre-existing T-101 health tests failed); State → Blocked-Escalated; cost $0.6861495. Javier must decide whether the frozen additive contract stands and authorize test-author updates to `conformance/app/tests/health.test.js`, or send T-102 back to planner for a revised contract.\n- 2026-07-12: operator decision — the frozen additive T-102 contract stands; test-author is authorized to update only the exact-match `/health` expectations in `conformance/app/tests/health.test.js` to include the frozen `approvals` object; State → In progress.\n- 2026-07-12: dispatch stopped because mandatory `next-stage.sh --ticket T-102` returned `RUN reviewer`, not `RUN test-author`; State → Blocked-Escalated. Javier must reconcile the sequencer/bookkeeping so it names the authorized test-author correction; no role run was launched.\n- 2026-07-12: operator resolution — no state surgery is needed; the frozen additive contract stands, the stale pre-T-102 exact-match assertions in `conformance/app/tests/health.test.js` are the fault, and `RUN reviewer` is the legal adjudication step; State → In progress.\n- 2026-07-12 21:14:32: reviewer round 1: REQUEST CHANGES — update only the four stale exact-match `/health` expected bodies in `conformance/app/tests/health.test.js` to include the frozen `approvals` object with scenario-appropriate counts; implementation and new tests conform; cost $0.1776.\n- 2026-07-12: dispatch refused before the authorized test-author fix because the mandatory sequencer reported `REFUSE reviewer ran 1 time(s) but only 0 verdict(s) are logged on /Users/sofiagonzalez/Projects/software-factory/conformance/factory/tickets/T-102.md`; the verdict exists on the ticket branch in `a2aba87`, but the dispatcher cannot write it to main; State → Blocked-Escalated. Javier must reconcile the main ticket bookkeeping or sequencer source; no role run was launched.\n- 2026-07-12: operator resolution — during T-102 runs, ticket bookkeeping is read from the ticket worktree while the ledger remains in the factory repo; use `FACTORY_ROOT=~/Projects/sf-worktrees/T-102/conformance` with `FACTORY_LEDGER=~/Projects/software-factory/conformance/factory/ledger.csv` for the rest of T-102; State → In progress.\nconst { test, before, after } = require(\"node:test\");\nconst assert = require(\"node:assert\");\nconst { spawn } = require(\"node:child_process\");\nconst fs = require(\"node:fs\");\nconst path = require(\"node:path\");\nconst os = require(\"node:os\");\n\nconst PORT = 4721;\nconst BASE = `http://localhost:${PORT}`;\nconst DATA_DIR = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), \"relay-health-test-\"));\nconst SERVER = path.join(__dirname, \"..\", \"server.js\");\nlet proc;\n\nconst post = (body) => fetch(`${BASE}/webhook/event`, {\n  method: \"POST\",\n  headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" },\n  body: JSON.stringify(body),\n});\nconst getHealth = async () => (await fetch(`${BASE}/health`)).json();\nconst getState = async () => (await fetch(`${BASE}/api/state`)).json();\n\nasync function settle(pred, tries = 60) {\n  for (let i = 0; i < tries; i++) {\n    const state = await getState();\n    if (pred(state)) return;\n    await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 100));\n  }\n  throw new Error(\"job did not settle\");\n}\n\nbefore(async () => {\n  proc = spawn(process.execPath, [SERVER], {\n    env: { ...process.env, PORT: String(PORT), DATA_DIR, WORKER_MS: \"50\" },\n    stdio: \"ignore\",\n  });\n  for (let i = 0; i < 50; i++) {\n    try {\n      if ((await fetch(`${BASE}/health`)).ok) return;\n    } catch { /* not up yet */ }\n    await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 100));\n  }\n  throw new Error(\"server did not come up\");\n});\n\nafter(() => {\n  proc?.kill(\"SIGKILL\");\n  fs.rmSync(DATA_DIR, { recursive: true, force: true });\n});\n\ntest(\"1. on a fresh server, /health reports an empty queue\", async () => {\n  const response = await fetch(`${BASE}/health`);\n  assert.strictEqual(response.status, 200);\n  assert.deepStrictEqual(await response.json(), {\n    ok: true,\n    queue: { pending: 0, done: 0, dead: 0 },\n  });\n});\n\ntest(\"1b. after an event is accepted, /health reports queue.pending incremented by 1\", async () => {\n  const pendingPort = 4722;\n  const pendingBase = `http://localhost:${pendingPort}`;\n  const pendingDataDir = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), \"relay-health-pending-test-\"));\n  const pendingProc = spawn(process.execPath, [SERVER], {\n    env: { ...process.env, PORT: String(pendingPort), DATA_DIR: pendingDataDir, WORKER_MS: \"600000\" },\n    stdio: \"ignore\",\n  });\n\n  try {\n    for (let i = 0; i < 50; i++) {\n      try {\n        if ((await fetch(`${pendingBase}/health`)).ok) break;\n      } catch { /* not up yet */ }\n      if (i === 49) throw new Error(\"pending server did not come up\");\n      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 100));\n    }\n\n    await fetch(`${pendingBase}/webhook/event`, {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" },\n      body: JSON.stringify({ id: \"health-pending\", payload: {} }),\n    });\n\n    assert.deepStrictEqual(await (await fetch(`${pendingBase}/health`)).json(), {\n      ok: true,\n      queue: { pending: 1, done: 0, dead: 0 },\n    });\n  } finally {\n    pendingProc.kill(\"SIGKILL\");\n    fs.rmSync(pendingDataDir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n  }\n});\n\ntest(\"2. after a job completes, /health reports queue.done incremented by 1\", async () => {\n  await post({ id: \"health-done\", payload: {} });\n  await settle(state => state.jobs.find(job => job.eventId === \"health-done\")?.status === \"done\");\n\n  assert.deepStrictEqual(await getHealth(), {\n    ok: true,\n    queue: { pending: 0, done: 1, dead: 0 },\n  });\n});\n\ntest(\"3. after a job exhausts retries, /health reports queue.dead incremented by 1\", async () => {\n  await post({ id: \"health-dead\", payload: { failTimes: 99 } });\n  await settle(state => state.jobs.find(job => job.eventId === \"health-dead\")?.status === \"dead\");\n\n  assert.deepStrictEqual(await getHealth(), {\n    ok: true,\n    queue: { pending: 0, done: 1, dead: 1 },\n  });\n});\n{\n  \"name\": \"relay-conformance\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"description\": \"Nysa-shaped conformance product for the software factory kit. Zero dependencies.\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"start\": \"node server.js\",\n    \"test\": \"node --test tests/conformance.test.js\"\n  }\n}\n3937ca6 (HEAD -> ticket/T-102-health-approvals-summary) Record T-102 worktree sequencer resolution\n5f80833 Escalate T-102 sequencer verdict refusal\na2aba87 Record T-102 reviewer round 1 verdict\n1886131 Record T-102 reviewer adjudication path\n4da596f Escalate T-102 sequencer mismatch\n858a190 Record T-102 operator contract decision\ndf1b829 Escalate T-102 test contract conflict\n5e201e0 T-102: implement approvals summary on GET /health; flag T-101 test conflict\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
